course: dictionary
lesson: 9677
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Context, the words and sentences around it, tells you which meaning the writer means. «An octopus has eight arms with suckers all along them.» Here a sucker is a body part that sticks to things, because the arms are being described.
explain: «Ha, sucker!» shouts Sam, after Tom falls for a trick. Now a sucker is a person who is easily tricked. The clue is the trick. This meaning is informal and often used to tease, so think about how the speaker feels.
explain: «I'm a sucker for chocolate» does not mean chocolate sticks to you. It means you cannot resist it. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A sucker is a part of an animal that sticks to things by suction, like the round pads on an octopus's arms.
fact: A sucker is also a rubber cup that sticks to smooth surfaces when you press it, like a suction cup on a window.
fact: «A sucker» can mean a person who is easily tricked. This is informal, and it is often used to tease.
fact: «To be a sucker for something» means you like it so much you cannot resist it.
fact: «Suckered into» means tricked into doing something. «Sucker punch» means a sneaky hit.
exercise: «An octopus has eight arms with suckers all along them.» What are the suckers here? | Tiny eggs | *Round pads it uses to grip | Long fins for swimming | Hard shells on its back
exercise: «Ha, sucker!» shouts Sam, after Tom falls for the trick. What does sucker mean here? | A rubber cup for the bath | A new shoot growing from a plant | *A person who is easily tricked | A lollipop
exercise: «I'm a sucker for chocolate.» What does this mean? | I always forget about chocolate | I do not like chocolate at all | *I cannot resist chocolate because I love it so much | I am allergic to chocolate
test: «Press the rubber sucker onto the window and it will stick.» What is a sucker here? | A word for teasing someone | A tiny fish | *A rubber cup that sticks to smooth surfaces | A root of a tree
test: Which phrase means tricked into doing something? | *Suckered into | Octopus suckers | Suction cup | Sucker punch
exam: «Remoras stick to sharks with a sucker on their heads.» Sucker here means: | A new shoot growing from a plant | *A body part that sticks by suction | A lollipop | A person who is easily tricked
